problems.problem:
King Arthur once had to fight the Dragon with Three Heads and Three Tails. His task became easier when he obtained a magic sword which could, with a single blow, do one (and only one) of the following: cut off one head; cut off two heads; cut off one tail; cut off two tails. Moreover, the Fairy Morgana revealed to him the dragon's secret:

- if one head is cut off, a new one grows;

- if two heads are cut off, nothing happens;

- in place of one tail, two new tails grow;

- if two tails are cut off, a new head grows;

- the dragon dies if it loses all three heads and all three tails.

How many blows are needed to kill the dragon?

A set of $n$ lamps, numbered from $1$ to $n$, are initially off. At each moment, it is possible to perform one of the following operations:

• change the state of lamp $1$;
• change the state of lamp $2$, provided lamp $1$ is on;
• change the state of lamp $k > 2$, provided lamp $k-1$ is on and all the lamps $1, \dots, k-2$ are off.

Show that it is possible, after a certain number of operations, to have only lamp $n$ on.
